<title>LUXEMBOURG: EU ministers press for 1998 cereals set-aside rate.</title>
<headline>EU ministers press for 1998 cereals set-aside rate.</headline>
<dateline>LUXEMBOURG 1997-06-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>European Union farm ministers called on Monday for the 1998 cereals set-aside rate to be set as quickly as possible so that farmers could plan next season's plantings, EU officials said.</p>
<p>At a meeting to fix 1997/98 (July/June) farm prices, France called for a zero rate while, at the other extreme, Germany and Austria called for 10 percent.</p>
<p>Britain sought an unchanged rate of five percent, a position shared by most EU countries.</p>
<p>But EU Farm Commissioner Franz Fischler said no decision could be taken for the time being.</p>
<p>&quot;It's a market management instrument. We should wait until we have all the necessary data,&quot; Fischler's spokesman quoted him as saying.</p>
<p>Fischler is expected next month to present a proposal for the 1998 set-aside rate.</p>
</text>
